I presume most of us who participated in or watched the \acro{TUG} 2020
conference are not professional historians but rather are computing
practitioners or users of computing technology such as \TeX\ and friends. We
have access to memories, papers, and flexibility in what we study,
and how we present what we learn, that won't all be available to
professional historians. I believe it is our job to help the
computing history world capture more computing history while it still
exists to be captured.
